/* Lesson quizzes — batch 11 */
window.LQ=window.LQ||{};
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p27m1":[
 {id:"p27m1q1", q:"A data-center leaf has 40 server ports at 25 Gbps and one 100 Gbps uplink to each of four spines. What is its oversubscription ratio, and what does it become when a fifth spine is added (one more uplink per leaf)?", options:["2.5:1, unchanged, because only one spine path forwards at a time","10:1 either way, because each server reaches only one 100 Gbps uplink","2.5:1, then 2:1","0.4:1, then 0.5:1"], answer:2, why:"Access capacity is 40 × 25 = 1,000 Gbps against 4 × 100 = 400 Gbps of uplink, so 2.5:1; a fifth spine makes 500 Gbps, so 2:1. Spine-leaf spreads traffic over every spine with equal-cost multipath, not one Spanning Tree path, so each spine adds capacity."},
 {id:"p27m1q2", q:"A 24-port PoE+ access switch has a 370 W PoE budget. The plan: 14 IP phones drawing 7 W, 6 Wi-Fi 6 APs at 25.5 W each at the device, and 4 cameras at 13 W. Does it fit?", options:["Yes: about 303 W, leaving roughly 67 W of headroom","No: 24 PoE+ devices at 30 W each need 720 W","Yes: about 196 W, since phones and cameras use a separate budget","No: the APs alone need 802.3bt Type 4 at 90 W each"], answer:0, why:"Budget by each device's actual class: 14 × 7 = 98 W, 6 × 25.5 = 153 W, 4 × 13 = 52 W, total 303 W of 370 W. Charging every port the PoE+ maximum of 30 W overstates the load; show power inline shows the real allocation."},
 {id:"p27m1q3", q:"Two wiring closets are joined by an existing 350 m run of OM3 multimode. With 10GBASE-SR optics in SFP+ slots the link logs errors constantly. Which change is the sound fix?", options:["Replace both optics with 10GBASE-LR and keep the OM3","Pull Cat 6A between the closets and use 10GBASE-T","Clean and re-terminate the connectors, since OM3 carries 10GBASE-SR to 550 m","Pull OM4 (rated 400 m for 10GBASE-SR) or single-mode with LR optics"], answer:3, why:"OM3 is rated for 10GBASE-SR to 300 m and OM4 to 400 m, so 350 m is past OM3's reach. LR optics are single-mode parts and must be matched to single-mode fiber, and any twisted-pair run stops at 100 m."},
 {id:"p27m1q4", q:"To blank a used lab switch you type erase startup-config, then reload. IOS asks \"System configuration has been modified. Save? [yes/no]\" and you answer yes. What does the switch boot with?", options:["A blank configuration, since NVRAM was already erased","Its old configuration, because yes copied the running-config back to NVRAM","A blank configuration, with the old lines kept in RAM until the next power-off","Nothing: it stops in ROMMON because NVRAM is empty"], answer:1, why:"The running-config in RAM still holds every old line, and answering yes writes it back to startup-config, undoing the erase. Answer no and the switch boots with no startup-config into the setup dialog; an empty NVRAM never sends it to ROMMON."},
 {id:"p27m1q5", q:"You want to run Netmiko from your laptop against real IOS XE devices over SSH at home, at no cost and legally. Which lab fits best?", options:["Packet Tracer, since its IOS fidelity covers every CCNA command","GNS3 with IOS images downloaded from a file-sharing site","CML-Free on a VM you host, running real IOS XE images, up to five nodes","EVE-NG Community, which ships Cisco images with the download"], answer:2, why:"CML-Free runs genuine IOS, IOS XE and ASAv images, up to five nodes, without a license purchase, so SSH and Netmiko behave as in production. Packet Tracer simulates IOS and is not a real SSH target, and GNS3 and EVE-NG ship no Cisco images at all."}
],
"p27m2":[
 {id:"p27m2q1", q:"VLAN 20. SW1: priority 32,768, MAC 0001.aaaa.0001. SW2: priority 28,672, MAC 00d0.ffff.0002. SW3: priority 32,768, MAC 0000.0c00.0003. Which switch becomes root bridge, and with what bridge ID?", options:["SW3, because it has the lowest MAC address","SW2, with bridge ID 28,672","SW2, with bridge ID 28,692","SW3, with bridge ID 32,788"], answer:2, why:"The bridge ID compares priority plus VLAN first: SW2 has 28,672 + 20 = 28,692, lower than 32,788 for the others, so SW2 wins and MACs are never consulted. The lowest MAC only breaks ties between equal priorities, and the VLAN number is part of the value."},
 {id:"p27m2q2", q:"SW4 can reach the root bridge two ways: a direct 100 Mbps link, or a 1 Gbps link to SW5, which has a 1 Gbps link to the root. Which port becomes SW4's root port, and at what root path cost?", options:["The port toward SW5, with a path cost of 8","The direct port, cost 19, because it is fewer hops","The direct port, cost 100","The port toward SW5, with a path cost of 4"], answer:0, why:"Root path cost sums the port costs toward the root: the direct 100 Mbps link costs 19, while two 1 Gbps hops cost 4 + 4 = 8. STP picks the lowest cost, not the fewest hops, so the direct link's SW4 end becomes the blocking alternate port."},
 {id:"p27m2q3", q:"Four 1 Gbps links form an LACP EtherChannel with port-channel load-balance src-dst-ip. A backup server copies one large file to a single NAS across it, with nothing else running. Roughly how fast can that copy go?", options:["About 4 Gbps, since LACP sums all four members","About 1 Gbps, since one flow hashes to a single member","About 2 Gbps, since half the members are held as standby","About 4 Gbps, once the hash includes source and destination IP"], answer:1, why:"EtherChannel hashes each flow's addresses to one member, so a single source-destination pair stays on one 1 Gbps link. The 4 Gbps aggregate appears only across many flows, and this hash already uses both IPs; no hash setting splits one conversation."},
 {id:"p27m2q4", q:"An engineer enters ip dhcp snooping, ip dhcp snooping vlan 10 and no ip dhcp snooping information option. Within minutes VLAN 10 clients stop getting leases. The real DHCP server is reached through uplink Po1. What is missing?", options:["ip arp inspection trust on every access port","ip helper-address on each access port","port-security maximum 2 on Po1","ip dhcp snooping trust on Po1"], answer:3, why:"Once snooping is on, every port is untrusted, so the server's Offers and Acks arriving on Po1 are dropped exactly as a rogue server's would be. Trusting the uplink fixes it. ARP inspection trust concerns ARP, and helper-address belongs on a routed gateway interface."},
 {id:"p27m2q5", q:"On a Layer 3 switch with ip routing, interface vlan 30 has an IP address and no shutdown, yet shows down/down. show vlan brief lists VLAN 30 as active with no ports, and no trunk is up. Why is the SVI down?", options:["No port carrying VLAN 30 is up, and an SVI needs at least one","SVIs come up only alongside router-on-a-stick subinterfaces","ip routing takes effect only after a reload","VLAN 30 must also be the native VLAN of a trunk"], answer:0, why:"An SVI comes up only when its VLAN exists and at least one port carrying that VLAN, access or trunk, is up; connect a host or bring up a trunk that allows VLAN 30. ip routing works without a reload, and the native VLAN has nothing to do with it."}
],
"p27m3":[
 {id:"p27m3q1", q:"R1's table: S 172.16.0.0/12 via 10.0.0.1; O 172.16.8.0/21 via 10.0.0.5 (cost 50); D 172.16.8.0/22 via 10.0.0.9; S* 0.0.0.0/0 via 10.0.0.13. Where does R1 send a packet for 172.16.13.5?", options:["10.0.0.9, the EIGRP /22, the longest mask with AD 90","10.0.0.1, the static /12, which has the lowest AD","10.0.0.5, the OSPF /21","10.0.0.13, the default route"], answer:2, why:"The /22 spans 172.16.8.0–172.16.11.255, so it does not contain 172.16.13.5; the /21 spans 172.16.8.0–172.16.15.255 and is the longest prefix that matches. Administrative distance only breaks ties between identical prefixes, so the static /12 loses despite AD 1."},
 {id:"p27m3q2", q:"R1 has ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 203.0.113.1 and ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 198.51.100.1 5. The first ISP's core network fails, but R1's Ethernet link to 203.0.113.1 stays up. What happens to internet traffic?", options:["It shifts to 198.51.100.1 within seconds, since AD 5 marks the backup","It keeps going to 203.0.113.1 and is lost; the backup never installs","It load-balances across both default routes","It is dropped until OSPF withdraws the failed primary"], answer:1, why:"A static route stays installed while its next hop is reachable over an up interface; it notices nothing beyond the directly connected link. The floating route installs only when the primary leaves the table, so traffic is blackholed. No OSPF is involved in static routes."},
 {id:"p27m3q3", q:"From R1 to a server LAN, path A crosses two 10 Gbps links and path B crosses one 1 Gbps link. Which path does OSPF choose with the default reference bandwidth, and which after auto-cost reference-bandwidth 100000 on every router?", options:["B by default (cost 1 versus 2), then A (cost 20 versus 100)","A in both cases, because 10 Gbps links always cost less","Both equally by default, then A (cost 2 versus 10)","B in both cases, because it has fewer hops"], answer:0, why:"With the 100 Mbps default, every link of 100 Mbps or faster costs 1, so A sums to 2 and B to 1 and the slower path wins. With a 100 Gbps reference, 10 Gbps costs 10 and 1 Gbps costs 100, so A (20) beats B (100). Path cost is a sum of link costs, not a hop count."},
 {id:"p27m3q4", q:"An engineer types ipv6 route ::/0 fe80::2 on R1, and IOS rejects the command. What is wrong with it?", options:["IPv6 default routes must be written as ::/128","Link-local next hops are ambiguous, so the exit interface must be named","ipv6 unicast-routing must be disabled before adding static routes","IPv6 static routes may point only at exit interfaces, never at next-hop addresses"], answer:1, why:"fe80::/10 is present on every interface and unique only per link, so fe80::2 is ambiguous until the link is named: ipv6 route ::/0 GigabitEthernet0/0 fe80::2. ::/0 is the correct default prefix; ::/128 is the unspecified address, and global next hops need no interface."},
 {id:"p27m3q5", q:"DSW1: standby 10 priority 110, standby 10 preempt, standby 10 track Gi1/0/1 20. DSW2: standby 10 priority 100, standby 10 preempt. DSW1 is active when its uplink Gi1/0/1 fails. What happens?", options:["DSW1 stays active; tracking only logs the uplink failure","DSW1 stays active, because 90 still beats the default priority","DSW2 becomes active only after DSW1 reboots","DSW1 drops to 90, and DSW2 at 100 with preempt takes over"], answer:3, why:"Tracking subtracts 20 from DSW1's 110, leaving 90. DSW2's 100 is now higher, and because DSW2 has preempt it seizes the active role, so the gateway follows the working uplink. The default priority is 100, which 90 does not beat."}
],
"p27m4":[
 {id:"p27m4q1", q:"Extended ACL SALES-IN, inbound on the sales gateway: 10 deny ip 10.2.10.0 0.0.0.255 10.2.50.0 0.0.0.255; 20 permit tcp 10.2.10.0 0.0.0.255 host 10.2.50.10 eq 443; 30 permit ip any any. Sales users cannot reach HTTPS on 10.2.50.10. What is the fix?", options:["Remove line 30, which overrides the permit on line 20","Apply the ACL outbound instead of inbound","Re-enter the permit as sequence 5 so it precedes the deny","Add permit tcp any any established at the end"], answer:2, why:"ACLs stop at the first match. HTTPS to 10.2.50.10 matches the deny on line 10 before line 20 is read, so the specific permit must come first; a named ACL lets you insert it as sequence 5. Line 30 never sees this traffic, so it is not the cause."},
 {id:"p27m4q2", q:"Which ACL entry matches exactly the eight source addresses 10.4.4.16 through 10.4.4.23 and nothing else?", options:["permit ip 10.4.4.16 0.0.0.7 any","permit ip 10.4.4.16 0.0.0.8 any","permit ip 10.4.4.16 255.255.255.248 any","permit ip 10.4.4.16 0.0.0.15 any"], answer:0, why:"Eight aligned addresses form a /29, mask 255.255.255.248, and the wildcard is its inverse: 255 − 248 = 7, so 0.0.0.7 ignores the last three bits. Writing the subnet mask itself inverts the logic, and 0.0.0.15 spans sixteen addresses, 16–31."},
 {id:"p27m4q3", q:"A branch has 250 users in 10.20.10.0/24 behind R1 and one public address, 203.0.113.5, on g0/0. ACL 1 permits the user subnet, and ip nat inside/outside are set. Which NAT statement lets all users browse at the same time?", options:["A static NAT entry mapping each user host to 203.0.113.5","ip nat inside source list 1 interface g0/0 overload","A dynamic NAT pool holding only 203.0.113.5, without overload","ip nat outside source list 1 interface g0/1 overload"], answer:1, why:"PAT (overload) rewrites source ports as well as addresses, so hundreds of inside hosts share one public IP, each flow tracked as IP:port. A one-address pool without overload serves one host at a time, and static NAT cannot map 250 hosts to a single address."},
 {id:"p27m4q4", q:"R1 syncs to an internal NTP server, and that server syncs to a server with a GPS receiver attached directly. What stratum should show ntp status report on R1?", options:["Stratum 1, because the time still comes from GPS","Stratum 2, because R1 is a client","Stratum 16, because R1 is two hops from the source","Stratum 3, one more than its own server"], answer:3, why:"The GPS clock is stratum 0, the server attached to it stratum 1, the internal server that syncs to it stratum 2, and R1 one step further, stratum 3. Each client sits one stratum below its source. Stratum 16 means unsynchronized, not distant."},
 {id:"p27m4q5", q:"An access port serves an IP phone with a PC plugged into it. A user finds that marking his PC's traffic DSCP EF 46 speeds up his downloads across the congested WAN. What should the access port's QoS policy do?", options:["Trust every marking on the port so the PC's traffic gets priority too","Trust the phone's markings and re-mark the PC's traffic to 0","Re-mark everything, voice included, to 0 and add WAN bandwidth","Give EF its own queue, since host markings cannot be abused"], answer:1, why:"The trust boundary belongs at the phone: trust the markings it applies to voice and re-mark the PC behind it to 0, so only real voice reaches the strict-priority queue. Trusting everything lets any host claim EF and starve calls, and prioritization never creates bandwidth."}
],
"p27m5":[
 {id:"p27m5q1", q:"R1 has aaa authentication login default group tacacs+ local. ISE is reachable and answers, but rejects user admin because the account was disabled in Active Directory. A local username admin still exists on R1. What happens at login?", options:["Login fails; local is tried only if no TACACS+ server responds","Login succeeds, because local is tried after any TACACS+ rejection","Login succeeds from the console, which never uses AAA lists","Login fails until aaa new-model is removed"], answer:0, why:"A method list falls through to the next method only on an error, such as no server answering. An explicit reject from TACACS+ is a final answer, so the local account is not tried. Local fallback exists for an ISE outage, not to bypass a disabled account."},
 {id:"p27m5q2", q:"An audit finds enable password 7 0822455D0A16 in a switch's configuration, produced by service password-encryption. What is the risk, and what is the fix?", options:["None; type 7 is encrypted with a key held on the device","Type 7 reverses in seconds; use enable algorithm-type scrypt secret","Type 7 is a one-way hash; rotate the password monthly to stay ahead of cracking","Only console users see it; add exec-timeout 5 0 to the line"], answer:1, why:"Type 7 is a reversible obfuscation that free tools decode instantly, so anyone holding a configuration backup holds the password. enable algorithm-type scrypt secret stores a type 9 hash instead. Calendar rotation does not fix a reversible encoding."},
 {id:"p27m5q3", q:"On a new switch you set hostname ASW9 and create username admin with a secret, then on line vty 0 15 set transport input ssh and login local. SSH clients get connection refused, and show ip ssh reports SSH disabled. What is missing?", options:["ip domain-name, then crypto key generate rsa modulus 2048","transport input telnet, so the line can negotiate SSH","service password-encryption, so SSH can protect the password","aaa new-model, because SSH requires a TACACS+ server"], answer:0, why:"SSH needs an RSA key pair, and IOS names the key from the hostname and domain name, so set ip domain-name and generate a 2048-bit key, then ip ssh version 2. Telnet is plaintext and unnecessary, and login local works without any AAA server."},
 {id:"p27m5q4", q:"Every access port in a building uses one template. Employee laptops must land in VLAN 20, and printers, which cannot run 802.1X, in VLAN 60. Which design does that?", options:["Static access VLANs set per port by the help desk","Port security with sticky MACs, mapping each learned MAC to its own VLAN","802.1X with RADIUS returning the VLAN, and MAB for the printers","DHCP snooping, which assigns the VLAN from the lease"], answer:2, why:"RADIUS can return a VLAN and an ACL in its Access-Accept, so identity rather than port decides placement; printers fall back to MAC Authentication Bypass, an allowlist rather than real authentication. Static per-port VLANs break whenever a device moves."},
 {id:"p27m5q5", q:"A branch's eight lightweight APs run in local mode, tunneling all client traffic over a 20 Mbps WAN to the HQ controller, even traffic to the branch's own file server. When the WAN drops, Wi-Fi users lose everything. Which change fits?", options:["Put the APs in monitor mode so they keep listening during outages","Move CAPWAP data from UDP 5247 to the 5246 control tunnel","Use bridge mode to link the APs to each other over the air","Convert them to FlexConnect so client traffic switches locally"], answer:3, why:"FlexConnect switches client traffic at the AP onto the branch VLAN, so local traffic stays local and service survives loss of the WAN to the controller. Monitor mode stops serving clients entirely, and changing ports alters nothing about where traffic is switched."}
],
"p27m6":[
 {id:"p27m6q1", q:"After data = json.loads(text) on {\"site\": \"HQ\", \"switches\": [{\"name\": \"ASW1\", \"vlans\": [10, 20]}, {\"name\": \"ASW2\", \"vlans\": [30]}]}, what is data[\"switches\"][0][\"vlans\"][1]?", options:["30","10","20","The string ASW2"], answer:2, why:"switches is an array of two objects; index 0 is the ASW1 object, its vlans value is the array [10, 20], and index 1 of that array is the number 20. Indexes count from zero, so 10 is index 0, and 30 belongs to the second switch."},
 {id:"p27m6q2", q:"Using the same token, a script's GET /dna/intent/api/v1/network-device returns 200, but a DELETE on one device returns 403. What does that tell you?", options:["The token expired between the two calls; log in again","The account is authenticated but not authorized to delete","The device ID in the URI does not exist","The controller is rate-limiting the script"], answer:1, why:"403 Forbidden means the server knows who you are and refuses the action, typically because the role is read-only. An expired or missing token returns 401, a wrong resource 404 and rate limiting 429, and the GET just proved the token works."},
 {id:"p27m6q3", q:"An Ansible ios_config play sets two NTP servers. Night 1 it reports changed on 40 switches; night 2 it reports ok on all 40. On night 30 it reports changed on one switch. What most likely happened?", options:["The play is not idempotent and should be rewritten","Ansible re-pushes every line nightly, so changed is normal","That switch lost NTP sync, which Ansible detected","Someone edited that switch's NTP lines by hand: drift"], answer:3, why:"ios_config compares the intended lines with the running-config and pushes only differences, so a correct play reports ok once the state matches. A later changed means the device drifted from the source of truth and the run corrected it. Ansible checks configuration, not sync status."},
 {id:"p27m6q4", q:"Job 1: declare Meraki networks and a cloud VPC, preview changes against a recorded state, then apply. Job 2: push NTP lines to 300 IOS switches over SSH with no agent installed. Which tools fit?", options:["Terraform for job 1, Ansible for job 2","Ansible for job 1, Terraform for job 2","Puppet for both, since its agents pull configuration","Chef for job 2, since it is agentless"], answer:0, why:"Terraform is declarative: HCL resources, terraform plan against its state file, then apply, with Meraki and cloud providers. Ansible is agentless and pushes playbook tasks over SSH, which suits device CLI work. Puppet and Chef are agent-based pull tools the v1.1 exam dropped."},
 {id:"p27m6q5", q:"Classify three faults on one switch: (a) OSPF adjacencies keep resetting; (b) transit frames are dropped by the forwarding ASIC; (c) SSH and SNMP to the switch time out while traffic through it flows normally.", options:["a data, b control, c management","a management, b data, c control","a control, b management, c data","a control, b data, c management"], answer:3, why:"Routing protocols such as OSPF are the control plane, which decides how to forward. Moving transit frames through the ASIC is the data plane. CLI, SSH, SNMP and APIs are the management plane, which can fail while forwarding carries on, exactly as in (c)."}
],
"p27m7":[
 {id:"p27m7q1", q:"A user's PC has a valid 10.1.30.x lease. Ping to her gateway 10.1.30.1 succeeds; pings to 8.8.8.8 and to an HQ server both fail. Using divide and conquer, where do you look next?", options:["Her NIC and patch cable, since bottom-up starts at Layer 1","Her DHCP lease, since the address may be APIPA","Her browser's proxy settings, working top-down","Beyond the gateway: its routing table, uplinks and edge"], answer:3, why:"A successful gateway ping proves the cable, port, VLAN, addressing and gateway are working, so the fault lies past the gateway: show ip route on it, then the path toward the edge. Restarting bottom-up re-tests layers that one ping already cleared, and a valid lease is not APIPA."},
 {id:"p27m7q2", q:"show ip interface brief on R2 lists GigabitEthernet0/1 as up/down. A cable tester says the cable is good. What should you check first?", options:["Replace the cable anyway, since up/down always points to a Layer 1 fault","Layer 2 and the far end: encapsulation, keepalives or a disabled peer","Type no shutdown on R2's Gi0/1","The interface's IP address and subnet mask"], answer:1, why:"The first word, up, says Layer 1 has signal; the second, down, says the line protocol failed, so look at encapsulation, keepalives and the peer port. A bad cable shows down/down, no shutdown fixes only administratively down, and an IP address does not affect line protocol."},
 {id:"p27m7q3", q:"R1: router ospf 1, network 10.0.0.0 0.255.255.255 area 0, passive-interface default, no passive-interface GigabitEthernet0/0. R2 connects to R1's Gi0/1 in area 0 with matching timers, yet show ip ospf neighbor on R1 shows no R2. Why?", options:["Gi0/1 is still passive, so R1 sends no hellos on it","The network statement needs a separate /32 wildcard per subnet","The pair is stuck in EXSTART because of an MTU mismatch","Passive interfaces must finish a DR election first"], answer:0, why:"passive-interface default silences hellos on every interface, and only Gi0/0 was re-enabled, so the link to R2 never forms an adjacency; add no passive-interface GigabitEthernet0/1. An MTU problem would show R2 listed but stuck in EXSTART, not absent."},
 {id:"p27m7q4", q:"Over SSH to a remote router, you must apply a new access-class to the VTY lines. Which sequence protects you from locking yourself out?", options:["Apply it, copy run start, then reload in 10 as a safety net","reload in 10, apply, test a new SSH session, then reload cancel and save","Apply it, then run debug all to watch your own session being filtered in real time","copy run start, apply it, and reload if the session drops"], answer:1, why:"reload in 10 reboots into the last saved configuration unless cancelled, so a lockout undoes itself. Save only after a fresh session proves access; saving first makes the bad ACL survive the reload, and once locked out you cannot type reload at all."},
 {id:"p27m7q5", q:"Suppose your CCNA has 100 items in 120 minutes, five of them labs you budget at 6 minutes each. Spreading the remaining time evenly, about how long can each other item take?", options:["About 72 seconds","About 54 seconds","About 57 seconds","About 38 seconds"], answer:2, why:"The labs take 5 × 6 = 30 minutes, leaving 90 minutes for 95 other items: 90 × 60 ÷ 95 ≈ 57 seconds each. Dividing 120 minutes by all 100 items (72 s) ignores the labs, and dividing 90 minutes by 100 (54 s) counts the labs twice."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p28m1":[
 {id:"p28m1q1", q:"Changing SNMP settings on 250 routers takes a careful engineer about 6 minutes each, with an error rate near 2 percent per device. A script needs about 9 seconds per router and runs 15 sessions in parallel. How do the two compare?", options:["25 hours and about 5 errors by hand; about 2.5 minutes by script","25 hours and about 5 errors by hand; about 37.5 minutes by script","2.5 hours and about 50 errors by hand; about 2.5 minutes by script","15 hours and about 2 errors by hand; about 9 seconds by script"], answer:0, why:"By hand: 250 × 6 = 1,500 minutes, which is 25 hours, and 2% of 250 is 5 bad configurations. By script: 250 × 9 s = 2,250 s spread across 15 parallel sessions is 150 s. Forgetting the parallelism gives 37.5 minutes."},
 {id:"p28m1q2", q:"A team renders configurations from Jinja2 templates and pushes them with Ansible, check mode first. The template variables live in a spreadsheet on a shared drive, and engineers still hot-fix switches at the CLI during incidents. Where are they on the maturity model, and what moves them up?", options:["Level 2; move intended state into NetBox and Git, run CI, stop hand edits","Level 3; they already use infrastructure-as-code tools such as Ansible and Jinja2","Level 1; any CLI editing at all keeps a team at the read-only scripting stage","Level 4; adding gNMI telemetry to verify intent is the only remaining step"], answer:0, why:"Templated pushes with check mode are level 2. Level 3 needs intended state in a source of truth and Git, CI testing of every change, and devices that are never hand-edited. Owning IaC tools is not level 3 while a spreadsheet and CLI hot fixes hold the truth."},
 {id:"p28m1q3", q:"To keep NetBox current, a junior engineer schedules a nightly script that reads every switch's running VLANs and interface descriptions and writes them into NetBox. What is wrong with this design?", options:["Nothing; NetBox should mirror each device's operational state","It records drift as intended state, so drift becomes permanent","NetBox accepts writes only through GraphQL, not REST","Nothing, provided the script also commits the data to Git"], answer:1, why:"NetBox models desired state: automation reads it and pushes to devices. Copying running configurations into it makes the device the truth, so a 2 a.m. hot fix becomes the official design and no drift audit can ever find it again."},
 {id:"p28m1q4", q:"In a demo you declare: guest devices may reach only the internet. The system generates configuration for 200 switches, pushes it, and an hour later flags three switches where a guest reached an internal subnet. Which concept is this, and which step raised the flag?", options:["OpenFlow SDN; the flag came from activation","Controller-based management; the flag came from translation","Intent-based networking; the flag came from translation","Intent-based networking; the flag came from assurance"], answer:3, why:"Declaring an outcome that the system turns into configuration is intent-based networking: translation renders it, activation pushes it, and assurance checks reality against the intent with telemetry, which is what found the three switches. Translation happens before anything is deployed."},
 {id:"p28m1q5", q:"An audit of 80 switches finds 12 pointing at a retired NTP server and 5 with no logging host; 3 of those switches have both problems. What share of the switches has drifted?", options:["25%","15%","17.5%","21.25%"], answer:2, why:"Count switches, not findings: 12 + 5 − 3 = 14 distinct switches, and 14 ÷ 80 = 17.5%. Adding the overlap instead of subtracting gives 20 (25%), and 17 ÷ 80 = 21.25% counts the three doubly drifted switches twice."}
],
"p28m2":[
 {id:"p28m2q1", q:"An audit script spends about 6 seconds per switch, nearly all of it waiting on the network. With ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=20), roughly how long does a run against 120 switches take?", options:["About 12 minutes, because the GIL runs one thread at a time","About 6 seconds, since all 120 sessions open at once","About 36 seconds, versus 12 minutes sequentially","About 6 minutes, since threads only halve network waits"], answer:2, why:"120 ÷ 20 = 6 rounds of about 6 seconds, roughly 36 seconds, against 120 × 6 = 720 s one by one. The interpreter lock is released while a thread waits on I/O, so network-bound threads overlap; only 20 run at once, not 120."},
 {id:"p28m2q2", q:"ntp.cfg holds the two correct ntp server lines. A router also carries a stale ntp server 10.9.9.9 that is not in the file. You run load_merge_candidate(filename=\"ntp.cfg\"), compare_config() and commit_config(). What is left on the router?", options:["Only the two correct servers, since a merge removes lines not in the file","The two correct servers plus the stale 10.9.9.9, since a merge only adds","No change at all, because compare_config() always returns an empty diff","Only the stale server, because commit reverts to the archived config"], answer:1, why:"A merge candidate adds or changes the lines it contains and leaves everything else alone, so the stale server survives. Removing it takes load_replace_candidate with a complete golden configuration, or an explicit no ntp server 10.9.9.9 line in the merge file."},
 {id:"p28m2q3", q:"Your 50-switch audit stops at switch 17 with an unhandled NetmikoTimeoutException, and switches 18 to 50 never run. What is the right fix?", options:["Raise conn_timeout to 300 s so slow hosts eventually answer","Delete switch 17 from the inventory and rerun the audit","Retry switch 17 in a loop until it finally responds","Catch the exception, record it as unreachable, and continue"], answer:3, why:"Catch the specific Netmiko exceptions, record the failure as data in that switch's row and keep going, with a short conn_timeout so a dead host costs seconds. A 300 s timeout makes every dead host cost five minutes, and deleting it hides a real finding."},
 {id:"p28m2q4", q:"rows = conn.send_command(\"show ip interface brief\", use_textfsm=True) returns a list of dicts with keys interface, ip_address, status and proto. Which expression lists the names of interfaces whose status is down?", options:["[r[\"interface\"] for r in rows if r[\"status\"] == \"down\"]","[r[\"status\"] for r in rows if r[\"interface\"] == \"down\"]","[rows[\"interface\"] for r in rows if r[\"status\"] == \"down\"]","[r[\"interface\"] for r in rows if \"down\" in rows]"], answer:0, why:"TextFSM returns one dictionary per row, so you iterate the rows, test each row's status key and collect its interface key. Indexing the list itself with a string raises TypeError, and \"down\" in rows asks whether the list contains that string, which it never does."},
 {id:"p28m2q5", q:"A colleague clones your audit repository, runs pip install netmiko into the system Python, and the script fails because newer Netmiko and ntc-templates versions return different fields. What should the repository have given her?", options:["The .venv directory committed to Git, so she gets your exact packages","A requirements.txt from pip freeze, to install into her own venv","A note telling her to upgrade to the latest Netmiko release first","A sudo pip install line so the packages install system-wide"], answer:1, why:"pip freeze pins the exact versions you tested; she creates a venv and runs pip install -r requirements.txt to reproduce them. A .venv is machine-specific and never belongs in Git, and global or latest installs are what caused the mismatch."}
],
"p28m3":[
 {id:"p28m3q1", q:"A script sends POST /networks/N_24/vlans to create VLAN 40, and the connection times out before any response arrives. A colleague wants the script to retry automatically. What is the safe approach?", options:["Retry the POST at once, since POST is idempotent by design","GET the VLAN list first, and POST only if VLAN 40 is absent","Send DELETE for VLAN 40, then POST again to reset the state","Retry until a 201 arrives, however many attempts it takes"], answer:1, why:"POST is not idempotent: the first request may have succeeded before the timeout, so a blind retry can create a duplicate or hit 409 Conflict. Checking state first makes the operation safe to repeat. A timed-out PUT, by contrast, could simply be resent."},
 {id:"p28m3q2", q:"A POST to a Catalyst Center intent endpoint returns 202 Accepted with a body containing a taskId, but the device is not yet provisioned. What should the script do next?", options:["Repeat the POST until it returns 201 Created","Treat 202 as a failure and open a ticket","Poll the task by its ID until it reports the outcome","Wait for the token to expire, then log in and retry"], answer:2, why:"202 means the request was accepted and is running asynchronously; Catalyst Center hands back a task ID that you poll for success or failure. Re-sending the POST could start the same work twice, and nothing about a 202 signals an error."},
 {id:"p28m3q3", q:"A Meraki inventory script requests /organizations/{id}/devices with perPage=1000 and reports exactly 1,000 devices. The organization has 2,350. What is the likely bug?", options:["Meraki caps every organization's device list at 1,000","The script hit 429 and the API silently truncated the list","perPage should be 2,350 so every device fits in one reply","It never follows the Link header's rel=\"next\" URL"], answer:3, why:"Exactly one page's worth is the signature of pagination: the first reply holds 1,000 devices plus a Link header naming the next page, which must be followed until none remains, three calls here. perPage has a maximum of 1,000, so it cannot simply be raised."},
 {id:"p28m3q4", q:"A lab script sends Authorization: Basic YWRtaW46QzFzY28xMjM= over plain http:// to a controller. What can someone capturing that traffic learn?", options:["Nothing, because base64 encrypts the credentials","Only the username, since the password part is hashed","Nothing without the controller's private TLS key","Both, by decoding the base64: admin and C1sco123"], answer:3, why:"Base64 is an encoding that anyone can reverse, not encryption, so a capture of plain HTTP yields admin:C1sco123 immediately. Basic auth is acceptable only inside TLS, and over http:// there is no TLS session or key protecting anything."},
 {id:"p28m3q5", q:"A partner's reporting app needs read-only access to your team's Webex data. You do not want to share any password, and you want to be able to revoke the app alone later. Which mechanism fits?", options:["OAuth 2.0, granting the app a scoped, revocable token","Basic auth with a dedicated service account password","Your personal API key, pasted into the partner's config","A bearer token copied from your own browser session"], answer:0, why:"OAuth 2.0 lets a user grant an application scoped access without sharing a password, and that grant can be revoked on its own. Handing over a password or your own key gives the partner everything you can do, and revoking it breaks your own access too."}
],
"p28m4":[
 {id:"p28m4q1", q:"pyang -f tree prints: +--rw vlans / +--rw vlan* [id] / +--rw id uint16 / +--rw name? string / +--ro status enumeration / +--rw ports* string. Which statement is correct?", options:["Entries are keyed by id, name is optional, and status is read-only state","Entries are keyed by name, and status can be set with edit-config","ports holds a single string, and id is optional in each entry","vlans is a list, and each vlan is a leaf holding a uint16"], answer:0, why:"[id] names the list key, ? marks name as optional, and ro means config false, so status is operational state that edit-config cannot set. The * after ports makes it a leaf-list, a set of strings, and vlans is a container, not a list."},
 {id:"p28m4q2", q:"Which RESTCONF URL reads only the description of GigabitEthernet2 from the ietf-interfaces model?", options:["https://HOST/restconf/data/ietf-interfaces:interfaces/GigabitEthernet2/description","https://HOST/restconf/data/ietf-interfaces:interfaces/interface=GigabitEthernet2/description","https://HOST/restconf/data/ietf-interfaces:interfaces/interface[name=GigabitEthernet2]/description","https://HOST/api/v1/ietf-interfaces/interface/GigabitEthernet2?field=description"], answer:1, why:"RESTCONF URLs follow the YANG path: /restconf/data/, then module:container, then list=key, then the leaf, giving interface=GigabitEthernet2/description. Skipping the list name breaks the path, and [name=...] is gNMI path syntax, not RESTCONF."},
 {id:"p28m4q3", q:"You stream 10 counters per interface from 150 switches with 24 ports each. gNMI samples every 15 seconds, and the store needs about 40 bytes per value. What rate and daily storage result?", options:["120 values per second, about 415 MB per day","36,000 values per second, about 124 GB per day","2,400 values per second, about 8.3 GB per day","2,400 values per second, about 830 MB per day"], answer:2, why:"150 × 24 × 10 = 36,000 values per sample; ÷ 15 s = 2,400 per second; × 40 bytes × 86,400 s ≈ 8.3 GB per day. 120 per second is what five-minute SNMP polling would deliver, and 36,000 per second forgets to divide by the interval."},
 {id:"p28m4q4", q:"You must learn within about a second when any of 9,600 interfaces changes operational status, without streaming every value every few seconds. Which subscription fits?", options:["gNMI Subscribe in STREAM mode, ON_CHANGE on the oper-status path","gNMI Subscribe in STREAM mode, SAMPLE every second on all counters","gNMI Subscribe in ONCE mode, rerun from cron every minute","SNMP polling of ifOperStatus at a five-minute interval"], answer:0, why:"ON_CHANGE sends nothing until the value changes and then reports within about a second, which suits link state. One-second sampling of every counter floods the collector, ONCE is a one-shot read, and five-minute polling misses everything between polls."},
 {id:"p28m4q5", q:"After an IOS XE upgrade, your TextFSM-based audit reports zero interfaces on 30 switches, and no error is raised. Which change removes this whole class of failure?", options:["Raise Netmiko's read timeout so the full output arrives","Loosen the template's regexes to match any spacing","Pin the terminal width so the output format cannot shift","Read interfaces through a YANG model over NETCONF or RESTCONF"], answer:3, why:"Screen scraping bets that the output text never changes, and when it does, the template silently matches nothing. A YANG model is a typed contract with named paths, so structured data arrives with nothing to parse. Looser regexes and fixed widths only patch one release."}
],
"p28m5":[
 {id:"p28m5q1", q:"sw4 has VLAN 10 USERS, VLAN 20 voice and VLAN 30 CAMERAS. A play runs cisco.ios.ios_vlans with config 10 USERS, 20 VOICE, 40 PRINTERS and state: merged. What is sw4's VLAN table afterward?", options:["10 USERS, 20 VOICE, 40 PRINTERS, with VLAN 30 deleted","10 USERS, 20 voice, 30 CAMERAS, since merges never rename","10 USERS, 20 VOICE, 30 CAMERAS, 40 PRINTERS","40 PRINTERS only, since merged replaces existing entries"], answer:2, why:"merged adds or corrects what you list and leaves unlisted VLANs alone, so VLAN 20 is renamed, 40 is added and 30 stays. Deleting VLAN 30 is what overridden would do, because it makes the whole VLAN table match the list."},
 {id:"p28m5q2", q:"Variables: vlans = [{vlan_id: 10}, {vlan_id: 20}, {vlan_id: 40}] and dist_switch = dsw-b. Template: description UPLINK-TO-{{ dist_switch | upper }} and switchport trunk allowed vlan {{ vlans | map(attribute='vlan_id') | join(',') }}. What renders?", options:["description UPLINK-TO-DSW-B / switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,40","description UPLINK-TO-dsw-b / switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,40","description UPLINK-TO-DSW-B / switchport trunk allowed vlan 10 20 40","description UPLINK-TO-DSW-B / switchport trunk allowed vlan [10, 20, 40]"], answer:0, why:"The upper filter turns dsw-b into DSW-B; map(attribute='vlan_id') pulls each number out of the list of dicts, and join(',') glues them with commas into 10,20,40. Without join you would print a list, not the comma-separated string IOS expects."},
 {id:"p28m5q3", q:"group_vars/access.yml sets ntp_server: 10.1.1.10, and host_vars/sw2.yml sets ntp_server: 10.2.2.10. sw2 is in group access. A template renders ntp server {{ ntp_server }}. What does sw2 receive?", options:["ntp server 10.1.1.10, because group values win","Both lines, one rendered from each file","An error, because the variable is defined twice","ntp server 10.2.2.10, because host values win"], answer:3, why:"Ansible gives host_vars precedence over group_vars, so a per-host exception overrides the group default without editing it. A variable holds one value, so the template renders a single line, and defining it at both levels is normal and raises no error."},
 {id:"p28m5q4", q:"Job A: operations staff who do not write Python must run a VLAN rollout from a web UI with an audit log. Job B: a Python team wants a threaded audit they can step through in a debugger. Job C: create VPCs, subnets and security groups and track what was created. Which tools?", options:["A Nornir, B Ansible with AWX, C Terraform","A Ansible with AWX, B Nornir, C Terraform","A Terraform, B Nornir, C Ansible with AWX","A Ansible with AWX, B Terraform, C Nornir"], answer:1, why:"Ansible's YAML suits colleagues who do not code, and AWX adds the web UI, role-based access and audit log. Nornir is plain Python with a threaded runner, so debuggers and real data structures work. Terraform declares cloud resources and tracks them in its state file."},
 {id:"p28m5q5", q:"A playbook's first real run reports changed=3 on three switches. Nobody else touches them, yet the second run reports changed=2, and the third changed=2 again. What does this tell you?", options:["Normal: change counts fall slowly as devices converge","Ansible always reports changed for network_cli tasks","A task is not idempotent and pushes on every run","The switches drift between runs and need locking"], answer:2, why:"An idempotent play reports changed=0 once the network matches the declared state. A steady changed=2 with no outside edits means some task pushes on every run because its intended text never matches the device. Fix it before scheduling the play as a compliance check."}
],
"p28m6":[
 {id:"p28m6q1", q:"Put these CI stages for a network pull request in order: Batfish analysis, lint, containerlab deploy with pyATS tests, render configurations, unit tests of templates.", options:["lint, unit tests, render, Batfish, lab tests","render, lint, lab tests, unit tests, Batfish","lab tests, Batfish, render, unit tests, lint","lint, render, lab tests, Batfish, unit tests"], answer:0, why:"Fail fast with the cheapest checks: lint in seconds, then template unit tests. Batfish analyzes rendered configuration files, so render must come before it, and the minutes-long lab deployment runs last, on a candidate that has already passed every offline check."},
 {id:"p28m6q2", q:"A pull request changes the branch edge ACL. The reviewer asks what the change will newly permit or block compared with today, and wants the answer before anything is deployed. Which check answers that?", options:["Batfish initIssues on the candidate, listing lines it could not parse","Batfish undefinedReferences on the candidate, listing missing objects","Batfish differentialReachability between current and candidate snapshots","A genie diff of pre- and post-change state learned from the routers"], answer:2, why:"differentialReachability compares two snapshots and reports flows the change newly permits or breaks, from configuration files alone. initIssues and undefinedReferences find parse errors and typos, not policy effects, and a genie diff needs live devices after the change is made."},
 {id:"p28m6q3", q:"A teammate committed the Ansible vault password in a file, noticed, and deleted it in the next commit. Both commits are already on the shared remote. What must happen?", options:["Nothing, since the latest commit no longer contains it","Rotate the vault password now, since it remains in history","Make the repository private, which removes the exposure","Revert the second commit so the history stays linear"], answer:1, why:"Every clone holds the earlier commit, so the password stays readable in history whatever later commits do; a secret that ever reached history is treated as leaked and rotated. Hiding the repository does not un-share what was already fetched."},
 {id:"p28m6q4", q:"After a canary deploy to one closet, the post-check genie diff shows an OSPF neighbor that was FULL is now missing, and Gi1/0/24 input errors rose by 4,000. Batfish and the lab tests had passed. What should the pipeline do?", options:["Continue the rollout, since Batfish approved the configuration","Pause for a week to see whether the counters settle down","Fail the post-check and roll back to the previous rendered config","Clear the counters and rerun genie diff until it passes"], answer:2, why:"pyATS verifies what the network is actually doing, and a lost adjacency fails the gate even though offline analysis passed. The planned rollback, NAPALM replace with the previous rendered configuration or a Git revert and redeploy, restores service; canary-first kept the damage to one closet."},
 {id:"p28m6q5", q:"Your CI runner must build a three-node lab on every pull request, push the candidate configuration, run pyATS tests and tear the lab down, with no human involved. Which lab fits?", options:["Packet Tracer, opened on the runner from a saved .pkt file","A rack of used Catalyst switches cabled to the CI server","GNS3, using the Cisco images bundled with its installer","containerlab, deploying a YAML topology of container NOSes"], answer:3, why:"containerlab builds a topology from one YAML file with one command, and because the nodes are containers it runs on a CI runner and tears down cleanly. Packet Tracer cannot be driven by outside scripts, and GNS3 ships no Cisco images at all."}
],
"p28m7":[
 {id:"p28m7q1", q:"A Meraki API key turns up in a public GitHub repository. In what order should you respond?", options:["Delete it from the repo, rotate it next quarter, then check the logs","Investigate who pushed it, then revoke it once misuse is confirmed","Revoke it, investigate, issue a new key, then review what it did","Make the repository private, then rotate at the next change window"], answer:2, why:"Revoke first so the exposed key stops working, then investigate, rotate to a new key and read the API's audit log for what the key did while exposed. Deleting or hiding the file leaves the key in history and in every clone, and waiting for proof of misuse gives an attacker time."},
 {id:"p28m7q2", q:"The nightly audit script and the deploy stage share one privilege-15 TACACS+ account. A laptop holding the audit script's .env file is stolen. What is the exposure, and what limits it?", options:["Low, since audits only read; rotating the password is enough","Write access everywhere; split read-only audit and CI-only deploy accounts","None, since TACACS+ encrypts the entire payload","Write access everywhere; fix it by adding MFA prompts to the shared account"], answer:1, why:"A shared account means the audit credential can reconfigure the network. Give the audit a read-only account limited to show commands and a separate deploy account held only in the CI secret store and usable from the runner's IP. Unattended scripts cannot answer MFA prompts."},
 {id:"p28m7q3", q:"A script calls Catalyst Center with requests.get(url, headers=hdr, verify=False) because the controller's certificate is issued by the company's internal CA. What is the right fix?", options:["Keep verify=False, since the traffic never leaves the internal network","Switch to plain http:// so that no certificate is involved","Suppress the urllib3 warning so the logs stay clean","Pass verify the path to the internal CA's certificate bundle"], answer:3, why:"Trusting the internal CA lets requests validate the controller's certificate, so an interceptor cannot pose as the controller and harvest the X-Auth-Token. verify=False accepts any certificate, on internal networks too, and plain HTTP exposes the token outright."},
 {id:"p28m7q4", q:"At 02:14:09 dist-3 logs an OSPF neighbor down. Which setup lets an engineer attribute it to a cause within seconds?", options:["Pipeline JSON logs and device syslog in one stack, with synced clocks","SNMP polls every five minutes, checked against the change calendar","Each script printing progress to the terminal of whoever ran it","Syslog kept only in each switch's local buffer, read on demand"], answer:0, why:"Correlation needs both streams, what automation did and what the network did, in one store with trustworthy timestamps, so change 4812 deployed at 02:14:07 sits beside the 02:14:09 event. Five-minute polls, terminals and local buffers miss or scatter that evidence."},
 {id:"p28m7q5", q:"For a lab you need to run NETCONF edit-config with full administrative rights on IOS XE for two days, without disturbing other users. Which option fits?", options:["An always-on DevNet IOS XE sandbox, since it needs no reservation","A DevNet reservation sandbox, reached over VPN with full admin rights","The read-only Meraki sandbox organization, which also runs IOS XE","Any public IOS XE router you can reach on TCP port 830"], answer:1, why:"Reservation sandboxes are private, last up to seven days and give full administrative access over VPN, so your edits affect no one else. Always-on sandboxes are shared with limited rights, and configuring a router you are not authorized to manage is a crime whatever port is open."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p29m1":[
 {id:"p29m1q1", q:"During a root-password reset you append rd.break, reach switch_root:/#, run chroot /sysroot and then passwd root, which fails with 'Authentication token manipulation error'. Which step did you skip?", options:["touch /.autorelabel, which has to come before passwd","mount -o remount,rw /sysroot, before the chroot","load_policy -i, since passwd needs SELinux loaded first","systemctl isolate rescue.target to unlock /etc/shadow"], answer:1, why:"rd.break leaves the real root mounted read-only at /sysroot, so passwd cannot write /etc/shadow until you remount it read-write before the chroot. The relabel flag comes after passwd and fixes the label on the next boot; it cannot make a read-only filesystem writable."},
 {id:"p29m1q2", q:"A client wants a free RHEL 10-compatible OS on a 2012 server whose Intel Sandy Bridge CPU lacks AVX2. Which choice will install and run?", options:["Rocky Linux 10, since it is bug-for-bug compatible","RHEL 10 under the free Developer Subscription","AlmaLinux 10, using its x86-64-v2 build","CentOS Stream 10, the stable long-term rebuild"], answer:2, why:"RHEL 10 and Rocky 10 require the x86-64-v3 baseline, which includes AVX2 (Haswell, 2013, onward), so a Sandy Bridge CPU cannot run them. AlmaLinux allows itself extra hardware support, including an x86-64-v2 build. CentOS Stream is the upstream preview of RHEL, not a stable rebuild."},
 {id:"p29m1q3", q:"You copy a set of RPMs into /var/www/html/repo on a lab server and point a client's .repo file at http://192.168.56.10/repo/. dnf on the client fails with: Cannot download repodata/repomd.xml. What is the fix?", options:["Set gpgcheck=0 so dnf skips the metadata check","Replace baseurl with a mirrorlist naming the same host","Run dnf clean all on the client and retry","Run createrepo_c on the repo directory on the server"], answer:3, why:"A repository is RPMs plus a repodata/ directory, and repomd.xml is its index; createrepo_c generates it. gpgcheck concerns package signatures, not missing metadata, and clearing the client's cache cannot fetch an index that does not exist on the server."},
 {id:"p29m1q4", q:"After root is moved onto a new storage controller, a server gets past GRUB and starts the kernel, then drops to a dracut shell saying the root device cannot be found. Where is the fault, and what fixes it?", options:["The initramfs lacks the controller's driver; rebuild it with dracut","GRUB2 cannot read grub.cfg; regenerate it with grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg","systemd's default target is wrong; fix it with systemctl set-default","shim failed the Secure Boot check; disable Secure Boot in firmware"], answer:0, why:"The kernel ran, so firmware, shim and GRUB did their jobs. The initramfs must carry the storage driver to find and mount the real root, so boot rescue media or a working entry and rebuild it with dracut. systemd's targets only come into play after switch_root."},
 {id:"p29m1q5", q:"A server rebooted unexpectedly overnight. journalctl -b -1 reports no previous boot, and /var/log/journal does not exist. What makes the next crash readable after the reboot?", options:["Run journalctl --since yesterday, which reads older boots","Enable rsyslog instead, since journald can never keep its logs across reboots","Raise the journal's in-memory size limit so it holds more","Create /var/log/journal or set Storage=persistent, then restart journald"], answer:3, why:"Without /var/log/journal, Storage=auto keeps the journal in /run/log/journal, which is lost at every reboot. Creating the directory, or setting Storage=persistent in journald.conf, and restarting systemd-journald preserves it. A bigger in-memory limit still lives in RAM."}
],
"p29m2":[
 {id:"p29m2q1", q:"On a saturated single core, a backup service runs with CPUWeight=50 beside a web service at CPUWeight=150. Roughly what share of that core does the backup get?", options:["50%, since its weight is set to 50","33%, a third of the web service's weight","25%, its weight over the total of 200","None until the web service goes idle"], answer:2, why:"CPUWeight shares a contended CPU in proportion: 50 ÷ (50 + 150) = 25%, and the web service gets 75%. It is not a percentage cap; that is CPUQuota. When the core is not contended, the backup may use whatever is idle."},
 {id:"p29m2q2", q:"Kerberos logins to a RHEL server start failing. chronyc tracking shows the clock about 420 seconds behind, and chronyc sources lists the lab time server with no ^* beside it. What is going on?", options:["Skew past Kerberos's 5-minute limit; restore the source, then chronyc makestep","Kerberos tolerates ten minutes of skew, so the fault must lie in the KDC","The time zone is wrong, and timedatectl set-timezone will fix the offset","chrony is healthy, because any listed source means the clock is in sync"], answer:0, why:"Kerberos rejects tickets more than five minutes off, and 420 s is seven. ^* marks the source chrony is actually using, so none is selected: fix the server or firewall path, then chronyc makestep to jump the clock. A time zone changes how time is displayed, not the offset."},
 {id:"p29m2q3", q:"A legacy daemon backgrounds itself: the process started by ExecStart forks a child and exits. With the default Type=simple, the unit goes inactive seconds after start and the daemon disappears. What is the fix?", options:["Type=oneshot with RemainAfterExit=yes","Restart=always with RestartSec=5","Type=forking with PIDFile= naming the daemon's PID file","Type=notify, so the daemon itself announces readiness to systemd"], answer:2, why:"Type=simple treats the ExecStart process as the service, so when it exits systemd considers the service finished and cleans up its cgroup, child included. Type=forking expects the parent to exit and tracks the child through PIDFile=. Restart=always would only loop the failure."},
 {id:"p29m2q4", q:"You need every message of priority warning or worse from sshd during the boot before the current one. Which command gives exactly that?", options:["journalctl -u sshd -b 1 -p warning","journalctl -u sshd -b -1 -p warning","journalctl -u sshd -b -1 -p info","journalctl -b | grep sshd | grep warning"], answer:1, why:"-b -1 selects the previous boot, while -b 1 counts from the oldest boot in the journal. -p warning shows that level and everything more severe, and -p info would add routine messages. Plain -b is the current boot, and grep cannot read the priority field."},
 {id:"p29m2q5", q:"A health-check job must run 10 minutes after every boot and then every hour after its last run. Which [Timer] settings express that?", options:["OnCalendar=hourly with Persistent=true","OnCalendar=*-*-* *:10:00","RandomizedDelaySec=10min with OnCalendar=hourly","OnBootSec=10min and OnUnitActiveSec=1h"], answer:3, why:"OnBootSec= counts from boot and OnUnitActiveSec= from the job's last activation, giving 10 minutes after boot and hourly after that. OnCalendar=hourly fires on wall-clock hours whenever the machine booted, and *:10:00 fires at ten past every hour, not ten minutes after boot."}
],
"p29m3":[
 {id:"p29m3q1", q:"alice was granted access with setfacl -m u:alice:rw- report.txt. Later someone runs chmod 640 report.txt, and getfacl now shows user:alice:rw- #effective:r--. Why can alice no longer write, and what fixes it?", options:["chmod set the ACL mask to r--, capping alice; setfacl -m m::rw- restores it","chmod deleted the ACL entry; re-run setfacl -m u:alice:rw- on the file","Named users never get more than the owning group; make alice the owner","The file needs a default ACL; run setfacl -d -m u:alice:rw- on it"], answer:0, why:"On a file with an ACL, the group bits shown by ls are the mask, so chmod 640 set the mask to r-- and capped every named entry; alice's entry survives but is effective r--. Restore the mask with setfacl -m m::rw- or chmod g+w. Default ACLs apply only to directories."},
 {id:"p29m3q2", q:"tyler belongs to wheel and devs. An admin runs usermod -G audit tyler, meaning to add him to audit. What happens to tyler?", options:["He ends up in wheel, devs and audit","His supplementary groups become audit alone, so he loses sudo","The command fails, because -G needs a comma-separated list","His primary group changes to audit"], answer:1, why:"usermod -G replaces the supplementary group list with exactly what you give it, so wheel and devs are gone, and with wheel goes full sudo. usermod -aG audit tyler appends instead. The primary group is set with -g, not -G."},
 {id:"p29m3q3", q:"Given Cmnd_Alias WEBCTL = /usr/bin/systemctl restart httpd, /usr/bin/systemctl status httpd, which line, in a file opened with visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/web, lets tyler run exactly those as root and nothing else?", options:["%wheel ALL=(ALL) ALL","tyler ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL","tyler ALL=(root) WEBCTL","tyler ALL=(root) /usr/bin/systemctl"], answer:2, why:"The alias names exactly the two permitted commands, so tyler ALL=(root) WEBCTL grants those and nothing more. A bare /usr/bin/systemctl allows every subcommand, including stopping sshd, and the wheel or ALL lines grant full root. visudo -f syntax-checks before saving."},
 {id:"p29m3q4", q:"A PHP app under httpd cannot reach its database at 10.0.0.5:5432. ausearch -m AVC -ts recent shows denied { name_connect } with scontext httpd_t and tcontext postgresql_port_t. What is the right fix?", options:["semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 5432","setenforce 0, since the denial proves SELinux is the cause","audit2allow, to build a module permitting the denial","setsebool -P httpd_can_network_connect on"], answer:3, why:"Outbound connections by httpd_t are governed by a Boolean, and setsebool -P flips it persistently. Port labels govern which ports a service may bind, not where it connects, and 5432 already has its own type. Permissive mode and audit2allow abandon or blindly widen the policy."},
 {id:"p29m3q5", q:"On a cloud RHEL 10 VM, /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/50-cloud-init.conf sets PasswordAuthentication yes. You add PasswordAuthentication no in 99-hardening.conf, run sshd -t and restart sshd, yet password logins still work. Why?", options:["sshd -t only checks syntax and never applies drop-in files","sshd keeps the first value read, and 50- loads before 99-; name it 01-","Drop-in files take effect only after a reboot, not a restart","PasswordAuthentication is honored only in /etc/ssh/sshd_config, never in drop-ins"], answer:1, why:"sshd reads the drop-ins in lexical order and keeps the first value it sees for each keyword, so 50-cloud-init.conf beats 99-hardening.conf. Naming yours 01-hardening.conf puts it first. sshd -t validates syntax, and a restart rereads every file without a reboot."}
],
"p29m4":[
 {id:"p29m4q1", q:"A volume group was created with vgcreate -s 16M. You run lvcreate -n lv_logs -L 100M vg_app. What size is lv_logs?", options:["100 MiB exactly, as requested","96 MiB, rounded down to 6 extents","128 MiB, rounded up to a power of two","112 MiB, rounded up to 7 extents"], answer:3, why:"Logical volumes are whole numbers of physical extents: 100 ÷ 16 = 6.25, so LVM rounds up to 7 extents, 7 × 16 = 112 MiB, and reports the rounding. It never rounds below the request, and extent sizes have nothing to do with powers of two in the volume size."},
 {id:"p29m4q2", q:"A 200 GiB XFS logical volume holds only 30 GiB of data, and another volume in the same group needs 100 GiB more. No new disk is available. What is a workable plan?", options:["lvreduce -r -L -100G while the volume stays mounted","Unmount it, then run lvreduce -r -L -100G offline","Back it up, recreate it at 100 GiB, restore, then grow the other","Run xfs_growfs with a negative size to shrink it in place while mounted"], answer:2, why:"XFS cannot shrink at all, mounted or not, so lvreduce -r has no filesystem tool to call and xfs_growfs only grows. The only path is to move the data off, recreate a smaller volume and restore. Shrinking with -r works only for ext4, and only offline."},
 {id:"p29m4q3", q:"/etc/auto.master.d/home.autofs reads /home/remote /etc/auto.home. Which line in /etc/auto.home mounts server1:/exports/home/<user> on demand for any user name?", options:["* -rw server1:/exports/home/&","& -rw server1:/exports/home/*","* -rw server1:/exports/home/*","/- -rw server1:/exports/home/&"], answer:0, why:"In an indirect map the key * matches whatever name is accessed under /home/remote, and & substitutes that same name into the export path. Swapping them reverses the roles, and /- marks a direct map in the master file, not a key inside this map."},
 {id:"p29m4q4", q:"After cryptsetup luksFormat /dev/sdc1 and cryptsetup luksOpen /dev/sdc1 secret, where do you create the filesystem, and what does fstab mount?", options:["mkfs.xfs /dev/sdc1, then fstab mounts that partition by the UUID blkid shows","mkfs.xfs /dev/mapper/secret; fstab mounts /dev/mapper/secret after crypttab opens it","mkfs.xfs /dev/mapper/secret, then fstab mounts /dev/sdc1 by its LUKS UUID","No mkfs is needed, since luksOpen presents a ready directory to mount"], answer:1, why:"The filesystem lives inside the opened mapping, /dev/mapper/secret; crypttab names the LUKS partition by its UUID and opens it at boot, then fstab mounts the mapping. Running mkfs on /dev/sdc1 overwrites the LUKS header and destroys the encrypted volume."},
 {id:"p29m4q5", q:"On /data, xfs_quota set bsoft=1g bhard=1200m for tyler, who now uses 1.15 GiB. He tries to write a 100 MiB file. What happens?", options:["It succeeds, since soft limits only warn","About 22 MiB is written, then the hard limit refuses more","Nothing is written, since he is already past the soft limit","It succeeds, since these limits count files, not blocks"], answer:1, why:"1.15 GiB is about 1,178 MiB, so only about 22 MiB remains under the 1,200 MiB hard limit, which then refuses writes. Being over the 1 GiB soft limit only starts the grace-period warning, and bsoft and bhard limit blocks, not file counts."}
],
"p29m5":[
 {id:"p29m5q1", q:"From server2, curl http://192.168.56.10:8443 fails. On server1, ss -tlnp shows nginx on 0.0.0.0:8443 and curl to localhost works. firewall-cmd --list-all shows 8443/tcp in public, but --get-active-zones puts enp2s0 in internal. What is wrong?", options:["nginx is bound to loopback, so remote clients are refused","The SELinux port label is missing, so nginx cannot bind 8443","The port is open in public, but enp2s0 is filtered by internal","server2 is resolving the wrong address for server1"], answer:2, why:"A listener on 0.0.0.0 rules out a loopback bind, and the working local curl rules out nginx itself. firewalld applies the zone bound to the interface, so open 8443/tcp in internal with --permanent and reload. The URL uses an IP, so names are irrelevant."},
 {id:"p29m5q2", q:"You fix DNS by adding nameserver 10.0.0.53 to /etc/resolv.conf. After the next reboot the line is gone. What is the lasting fix?", options:["chattr +i /etc/resolv.conf so nothing can rewrite it","nmcli con mod lab ipv4.dns 10.0.0.53, then nmcli con up lab","Add the nameserver line to /etc/hosts instead","Change nsswitch.conf to hosts: dns files"], answer:1, why:"NetworkManager generates resolv.conf from the connection's ipv4.dns setting and rewrites it on reconnect, so set the server on the profile and reactivate it. /etc/hosts holds name-to-address entries, not resolvers, and nsswitch order does not choose which DNS server is queried."},
 {id:"p29m5q3", q:"dig server2.lab.local returns 192.168.56.11, yet ping server2.lab.local goes to 192.168.56.99. /etc/nsswitch.conf says hosts: files dns myhostname. Why the difference, and which command shows what programs actually get?", options:["A stale /etc/hosts entry is read first; getent hosts shows it","ping caches old DNS answers; dig shows what programs get","DNS holds two A records, and ping picks one at random","myhostname overrides DNS; hostnamectl shows the result"], answer:0, why:"Programs resolve through nsswitch, which reads /etc/hosts before DNS, so a stale 192.168.56.99 line wins. dig queries the DNS server directly and bypasses nsswitch, while getent hosts follows the same path ping does. Fix or remove the /etc/hosts line."},
 {id:"p29m5q4", q:"A server has one NIC cabled to each of two separate access switches that are not stacked and have no LACP configured. The address must survive a cable or switch failure. Which aggregation fits?", options:["A bond in 802.3ad mode for full aggregation","A team device, the newer replacement for bonding","Two separate connections sharing one IP address","A bond in active-backup mode with miimon=100"], answer:3, why:"active-backup carries traffic on one port and fails over to the other within about a second, and it needs no switch cooperation, so it works across two independent switches. 802.3ad needs LACP configured on the switch side, and teaming was removed in RHEL 10."},
 {id:"p29m5q5", q:"server1 runs chronyd and syncs upstream. You point lab clients at it with server 192.168.56.10 iburst, but chronyc sources on the clients shows it unreachable. What does server1 still need?", options:["allow 192.168.56.0/24 in chrony.conf, and the ntp service open in firewalld","local stratum 1 in chrony.conf, since clients sync only to stratum 1","timedatectl set-ntp true on every client, followed by a reboot","An SELinux port label for UDP 123, added with semanage port"], answer:0, why:"chronyd serves time only to networks named in allow, and firewalld drops UDP 123 until you add the ntp service with --permanent and reload. Clients will sync to any stratum below 16, and port 123 already carries its NTP label in the default policy."}
],
"p29m6":[
 {id:"p29m6q1", q:"User app (UID 1002) has app:231072:65536 in /etc/subuid. A process inside its rootless container runs as UID 999. Which host UID does ps show for that process?", options:["232070","232071","2001","999"], answer:0, why:"Container root maps to the user's own UID 1002, and container UIDs 1 to 65536 map onto the sub-UID range starting at 231072, so UID 999 becomes 231072 + 999 − 1 = 232070. Forgetting the −1 gives 232071; adding to 1002 or keeping 999 ignores the mapping."},
 {id:"p29m6q2", q:"A rootless nginx container runs with -v /home/web/site:/usr/share/nginx/html and returns 403 for every page. The files are mode 644. ausearch -m AVC shows container_t denied read on user_home_t. What is the right fix?", options:["chmod -R 777 /home/web/site","Run the container with --privileged","setenforce 0 while the site is served","Recreate it with the volume suffix :Z"], answer:3, why:"The files carry user_home_t, which container_t may not read; :Z relabels the bind-mounted directory to container_file_t so the container can use it. The denial is SELinux, not file mode, so chmod cannot help, and --privileged or permissive mode discards the protection."},
 {id:"p29m6q3", q:"A script begins with set -euo pipefail and contains errors=$(grep -c ERROR /var/log/app.log). On a day with no ERROR lines the script exits at once with status 1. Why?", options:["grep -c prints nothing at all when there is no match","grep exits 1 when nothing matches, and set -e stops the script","pipefail treats every command substitution as a failed pipe","The variable errors is left unset, so set -u aborts"], answer:1, why:"grep -c prints 0 but returns exit status 1 when no line matches, and under set -e that failing assignment ends the script. Write errors=$(grep -c ERROR /var/log/app.log || true) when no match is acceptable. The variable does get assigned, so set -u is not the trigger."},
 {id:"p29m6q4", q:"Which command prints the names of accounts with a UID of 1000 or more whose login shell is /bin/bash?", options:["awk -F: '$1 >= 1000 && $7 == \"/bin/bash\" {print $3}' /etc/passwd","cut -d: -f1,3,7 /etc/passwd | grep bash","awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 && $7 == \"/bin/bash\" {print $1}' /etc/passwd","grep -E ':x:[0-9]{4}:' /etc/passwd | cut -d: -f1"], answer:2, why:"In /etc/passwd field 1 is the name, 3 the UID and 7 the shell, so testing $3 and $7 and printing $1 is right. Swapping $1 and $3 compares names with numbers, cut does not filter by UID, and the four-digit grep ignores the shell and misses five-digit UIDs."},
 {id:"p29m6q5", q:"In site.yml, the copy task for index.html has notify: Reload nginx. You run the playbook a second time, and index.html has not changed. What happens to the handler?", options:["It runs anyway, because handlers run at the end of every play","It runs, because notify fires whenever the task executes","It runs only if --check is passed on the command line","It does not run: copy reports ok, so nothing notifies it"], answer:3, why:"A handler runs only when a task that notifies it reports changed. On the second run copy finds the file identical and reports ok, so the reload is skipped. That is why handlers suit restarts: services bounce only when their configuration actually changed."}
],
"p29m7":[
 {id:"p29m7q1", q:"vmstat 1 shows si near 900 and so near 1,200 every second; free -h shows 80 MiB available; iostat shows the swap disk at 95% %util; load is 3.1 on 4 vCPUs. Which resource is starving, and what do you look for next?", options:["CPU, because the load is close to the vCPU count","Disk, since %util is 95%, so put a faster disk under the swap volume","Memory: swapping drives the disk, so find the memory consumer","Network, since %util includes remote I/O waits"], answer:2, why:"Non-zero si and so mean pages move to and from swap every second, and with almost nothing available, memory is the bottleneck; the busy disk is a consequence. ps aux --sort=-%mem names the culprit. A load of 3.1 on 4 vCPUs is not CPU saturation."},
 {id:"p29m7q2", q:"df -h reports / at 100%, but du over every directory adds up to about 60%. Yesterday someone deleted a 12 GB log with rm while the application was running. What explains the gap, and what reclaims the space?", options:["The filesystem metadata is corrupt; unmount / and run xfs_repair on it","A deleted log is still open; lsof | grep deleted shows what to restart","du skips hidden dot-files; rerun it with ls -a to find the missing usage","The journal reserves that space; free it with journalctl --vacuum-time=1d"], answer:1, why:"rm removes the name, but the blocks stay allocated while a process holds the file open, so df counts them and du cannot see them. lsof | grep deleted names the process; restarting it releases the space. Nothing is corrupt, and xfs_repair cannot run on a mounted root."},
 {id:"p29m7q3", q:"After dnf update, the new kernel panics at boot; the previous kernel still works. What is the sound recovery?", options:["Boot the install ISO, chroot into /mnt/sysroot and reinstall GRUB2 with grub2-install","Boot the previous kernel from GRUB, then remove the bad one or set grubby's default","Append rd.break and run dnf downgrade kernel inside the initramfs","Append enforcing=0, since kernel panics come from SELinux labels"], answer:1, why:"RHEL keeps three kernels (installonly_limit=3), so the previous one is in the GRUB menu; boot it, then dnf remove the faulty kernel-core or set the good one as default with grubby. GRUB is fine, since it loaded the new kernel, and a panic is not a label problem."},
 {id:"p29m7q4", q:"Users get 'No space left on device' when creating small files in /var/spool/app, yet df -h shows that filesystem only 40% full. What should you check?", options:["df -i, since the filesystem may have run out of inodes","free -h, since the page cache may be holding the space","The SELinux label on the directory, with ls -Z","The quota hard limit, since quotas show as 40% full"], answer:0, why:"Every file needs an inode as well as data blocks, and huge numbers of tiny files can exhaust the inodes while blocks remain free; df -i shows IUse% at 100%. An SELinux denial reads Permission denied, a quota reads Disk quota exceeded, and cache never blocks writes."},
 {id:"p29m7q5", q:"EX200, 25 minutes left. Every task is done except one storage task that is half finished, and you have not rebooted since editing /etc/fstab. What is the best use of the time?", options:["Finish the storage task first, since the reboot can wait until the final minute","Skip the reboot, since the grader checks only the running state","Revert to a snapshot and redo the storage task cleanly","Run mount -a, reboot, verify every task, then finish storage if time remains"], answer:3, why:"The grader checks state after a reboot, so an fstab mistake can leave the system unbootable and score zero everywhere. mount -a catches the typo, the reboot proves persistence, and per-task credit means a verified set beats one more finished task. The exam gives you no snapshots."}
]
});
